# How the standard library is built

The standard library is a package named `std`. Its modules are HQL files
of declarations, and its extension is Rust compiled into the `hql` binary.
A package you write has the same two halves; the one difference is where
its Rust is kept ([[35-how-a-library-is-loaded|chapter 35]]).

## The contract

A module's file of declarations is its **contract**. This is the head of
the contract of `math`, the file
[`lib/std/math/mod.hql`](https://github.com/ewiger/hql/blob/main/lib/std/math/mod.hql):

````text
module math

import core

/// `self` without its sign, at its own type: `abs(-3)` is `3`,
/// `abs(-1.50)` is `1.50`, and `abs(Duration("-PT1H"))` is `PT1H`.
fn abs(self: Int) : Int
fn abs(self: Float) : Float
fn abs(self: Decimal) : Decimal
fn abs(self: Duration) : Duration

/// The square root of `self`: `sqrt(2.0f)` is `1.4142135623730951`.
fn sqrt(self: Float) : Float
````

The file opens with `module` and its name, imports the modules whose types
it writes, and declares. A `///` doc string above a declaration is what
`help` prints for it. The contract is the whole of what a program is
checked against: the checker never reads Rust.

## The implementation

Beside the contract is one Rust function per declared name, and a table
that lists them by that name:

```rust
pub(crate) static STEPS: &[Operation] = &[
    declared("abs", eval_abs),
    declared("sqrt", eval_sqrt),
    // …
];

fn eval_abs(
    _: &mut dyn EvalCx,
    input: Value,
    _: &[TypedArg],
    span: Range<usize>,
) -> Result<Value, Diagnostic> {
    match number(&input, "abs", &span)? {
        Value::Int(n) => n.checked_abs().map(Value::Int).ok_or(Diagnostic::overflow(span)),
        Value::Float(x) => Ok(Value::Float(x.abs())),
        Value::Decimal(x) => Ok(Value::Decimal(x.abs())),
        _ => unreachable!("number() answers a number"),
    }
}
```

`eval_abs` takes the value the call is made on, the call's other
arguments, and the place of the call in the source, and gives a value or a
diagnostic. It serves all four forms of `abs` and reads which one it was
given from the value. The Rust states no HQL type: the types are the
contract's.

## Binding

**Binding** joins the two halves, once, before the first program is
checked. For each module of each package it pairs every declaration with
the function of the same name in that module's table, and it refuses the
package where the halves disagree:

| Defect | What binding reports |
| --- | --- |
| a declaration with no function | the declaration is declared, and the extension implements no such function |
| a function with no declaration | the extension implements a name no contract declares |
| `effectful fn` over a function marked pure, or the reverse | the two markings differ |
| a shape word that differs from the function's marking | the two markings differ |
| a type the contract writes and no module declares | the declaration states no signature |

Because binding runs before any program does, a package is never half
usable: either every declaration of its modules has its function, or the
package is refused whole.

## Reading the result

`hql builtins` prints what binding produced: each module, the package and
version that provide it, and each declared name with its forms, its purity
and its shape.

```sh
hql builtins
```

```text
math (std 0.7.0, prelude)
  abs
    Int | abs() -> Int
    Float | abs() -> Float
    Decimal | abs() -> Decimal
    Duration | abs() -> Duration
    pure, barrier
```

`Int | abs() -> Int` reads: on an `Int`, `abs` with no further arguments
gives an `Int`. The value before `|` is the receiver.
